1
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:11,280
Greetings and welcome to the MOC Introduction to Biarisk Management.

2
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:22,770
In this lecture, we will focus on Personal Protective Equipment, or PPEs.

3
00:00:22,770 --> 00:00:27,890
This is an example of PPEs as they are deployed in the field.

4
00:00:27,890 --> 00:00:34,370
These field workers are equipped with PPEs, which offer protection against different kinds

5
00:00:34,370 --> 00:00:36,190
of biological agents.

6
00:00:36,190 --> 00:00:40,810
This laboratory worker is wearing a complete set of PPEs.

7
00:00:40,810 --> 00:00:49,690
He has been equipped with respiratory protection, ocular protection, cutaneous protection in

8
00:00:49,690 --> 00:00:56,570
the form of his biosafety suit, as well as gloves and footwear.

9
00:00:56,570 --> 00:01:05,130
This worker, on the other hand, has exposed surfaces of his skin and this can constitute

10
00:01:05,130 --> 00:01:10,520
a potential breach of containment.

11
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:22,940
PPEs, as used by field workers, represent a last line of defense against biological agents.

12
00:01:22,940 --> 00:01:26,240
These are the learning objectives for this particular lecture.

13
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:31,040
In this lecture, you will learn about the different types of PPEs, learn how to select

14
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:36,660
PPEs based on risk assessment, learn about the standard operating procedures associated

15
00:01:36,660 --> 00:01:43,980
with PPEs, and learn about specialized PPEs.

16
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:47,340
The learning outcomes for this lecture are as follows.

17
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:51,460
Upon completion of this module, you should demonstrate the ability to describe the various

18
00:01:51,460 --> 00:01:59,020
types of PPEs, select PPEs to mitigate the risk posed by biological agents, understand

19
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:03,580
how administrative controls must be used concurrently with PPEs.

20
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:13,120
There are various types of PPEs.

21
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:19,520
These include masks for respirators, gloves, protective suits, protective eyewear and

22
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:25,310
specialized PPEs.

23
00:02:25,310 --> 00:02:32,750
Masks for respirators are designed to protect you against biological agents that are transmitted

24
00:02:32,750 --> 00:02:36,420
via aerosols.

25
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:43,900
In this case, the route of entry of the biological agent into the host is via the airway.

26
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:49,940
A wide range of masks are currently available and the N95 respirator is one of the most

27
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:56,620
widely used masks in the current situation.

28
00:02:56,620 --> 00:03:02,980
Another aspect which must be explained and explored is the fit testing of masks.

29
00:03:02,980 --> 00:03:10,220
Reuse of masks is not recommended as masks can be compromised when they are sterilized

30
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:13,580
using disinfectants.

31
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:17,760
There are standards for masks and you may refer to this particular standard from the

32
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:23,440
European Committee for Standardization in order to obtain an insight into the standards

33
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:28,600
for masks.

34
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:43,380
The N95 respirator is available in different models, the 8210, the 1860 and 1870+.

35
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:53,480
This is an example of the 1860S which protects you against particulates and can also be

36
00:03:53,480 --> 00:04:01,170
used as a surgical mask.

37
00:04:01,170 --> 00:04:08,450
The 3M model 8210 is designed to protect the wearer from exposure to airborne particles

38
00:04:08,450 --> 00:04:14,410
which include dust, mist, fumes, fibers and bio-aerosols such as viruses and bacteria.

39
00:04:14,410 --> 00:04:21,490
Please take note that this particular respirator can also be used to protect the user against

40
00:04:21,490 --> 00:04:28,250
non-biological agents such as dust and other particles which are airborne.

41
00:04:28,250 --> 00:04:34,250
N95s are designed to fit tightly on the face and create a seal between the user's face

42
00:04:34,250 --> 00:04:38,010
and the respirator.

43
00:04:38,010 --> 00:04:48,490
They meet certain requirements of the NIOSH 42 CFR 84 and N95 respirator will filter out

44
00:04:48,490 --> 00:04:59,410
a minimum of 95% of the solids and liquid aerosols that do not contain oil.

45
00:04:59,410 --> 00:05:05,270
One should take into account the fact that the N95 respirator has not been designed to

46
00:05:05,270 --> 00:05:14,980
protect the laboratory worker against aerosols that are borne on an oil or oil particulate.

47
00:05:14,980 --> 00:05:27,500
The N95 8210 cannot be used as a surgical mask and it is not resistant to fluids.

48
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:31,100
This is an example of a fit test.

49
00:05:31,100 --> 00:05:37,980
Now a fit test is a requirement when you select a respirator for your particular facial

50
00:05:37,980 --> 00:05:42,220
type or your face profile.

51
00:05:42,220 --> 00:05:51,580
Now the lady in this picture is wearing a respirator which has been designed for her

52
00:05:51,580 --> 00:05:54,020
facial profile.

53
00:05:54,020 --> 00:06:00,300
Now before she actually comments his work in the lab, this respirator must be fit tested

54
00:06:00,300 --> 00:06:10,140
and this fit testing is achieved by wearing this specific gear over the face and the biosafety

55
00:06:10,140 --> 00:06:21,100
officer which is this gentleman is using a nebulizer to create an aerosol in this particular space.

56
00:06:21,100 --> 00:06:29,860
Now this nebulizer may contain an agent which has a particular order and a particular taste

57
00:06:29,860 --> 00:06:38,380
and if the mask is effective, this user should not be able to detect the order or the taste

58
00:06:38,380 --> 00:06:45,900
of this particular aerosol and fit testing is an essential procedure which must be conducted

59
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:58,460
prior to selecting a mask to suit your particular facial profile.

60
00:06:58,460 --> 00:07:00,220
Gloves are available in many forms.

61
00:07:00,220 --> 00:07:06,140
We have the latex gloves, nitrile gloves as well as reinforced gloves.

62
00:07:06,140 --> 00:07:12,940
Now latex gloves are not recommended for usage with chemicals as they are permeable to many

63
00:07:12,940 --> 00:07:15,180
of the organic solvents.

64
00:07:15,180 --> 00:07:20,980
Nitrile gloves on the other hand are impermeable to solvents but must be used in accordance

65
00:07:20,980 --> 00:07:24,500
with their limitations.

66
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:30,180
Nitrile gloving is another procedure which is recommended for operations involving biological

67
00:07:30,180 --> 00:07:31,800
agents.

68
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:36,660
This is to mitigate any effect from spills.

69
00:07:36,660 --> 00:07:44,000
Reinforced gloves are recommended when working with animals such as mice in the laboratory

70
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:53,380
setting as animals tend to scratch and cause injuries to the skin.

71
00:07:53,380 --> 00:07:58,260
Protective suits are designed to protect the user from exposure to biological agents.

72
00:07:58,260 --> 00:08:01,620
They can be worn both in the laboratory and in the field.

73
00:08:01,620 --> 00:08:08,980
They will protect you against minor splashes but cannot be used to mitigate major spills.

74
00:08:08,980 --> 00:08:14,740
They involve a high cost and they are one-time use which means that they cannot be recycled

75
00:08:14,740 --> 00:08:20,650
after usage.

76
00:08:20,650 --> 00:08:26,250
Protective eyewear such as shields which are face shields are designed to protect the

77
00:08:26,250 --> 00:08:30,010
entry of the biological agent via the ocular route.

78
00:08:30,010 --> 00:08:34,530
They will protect you against minor splashes however if you are working with aerosols it

79
00:08:34,530 --> 00:08:45,580
is recommended that you use a powered air purifying respirator and not a face shield.

80
00:08:45,580 --> 00:08:49,820
Powered air purifying respirators are designed to protect the laboratory user from aerosols

81
00:08:49,820 --> 00:08:52,780
that can be generated in the laboratory.

82
00:08:52,780 --> 00:08:55,340
Let us look at the basic setup.

83
00:08:55,340 --> 00:09:02,500
In this case the upper portion of this respirator comprises the helmet and if you

84
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:05,780
look through the helmet which is transparent you will see the filter.

85
00:09:05,780 --> 00:09:07,780
This is the N95 filter.

86
00:09:07,780 --> 00:09:18,540
The air is directed over this filter through it and into this space over the user's face.

87
00:09:18,540 --> 00:09:24,140
Now the air flow generates a positive pressure in this region and the air exits through

88
00:09:24,140 --> 00:09:32,440
this apron and this creates a curtain of air around the user.

89
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:39,240
Now this curtain of air prevents the ingestion of aerosols via this particular route.

90
00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:44,040
So there is always a positive pressure of air which keeps the pathogens or the biological

91
00:09:44,040 --> 00:09:49,600
agents out of the user's facial area.

92
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:54,400
And this kind of powered air purifying respirator can protect you against aerosols as well

93
00:09:54,400 --> 00:10:02,080
as splashes during routine laboratory operations.

94
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:07,840
A video on the usage of the respirator can be accessed from this particular link.

95
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:18,990
I have prepared a link for you with a tutorial on the usage of the PAPR.

96
00:10:18,990 --> 00:10:25,550
Pressurized suits are generally used in a BSL4 laboratory and this pressurized suit

97
00:10:25,550 --> 00:10:31,390
is supplied with pressurized A via a tube.

98
00:10:31,390 --> 00:10:35,830
The suit is maintained as a positive pressure environment.

99
00:10:35,830 --> 00:10:41,070
In the case of a breach of containment, for instance a tear in the suit or a needle stick

100
00:10:41,070 --> 00:10:49,190
injury, the air will basically exit through the suit and prevent the intake of any kind

101
00:10:49,190 --> 00:10:51,830
of biological agent.

102
00:10:51,830 --> 00:10:58,670
Pressurized suits are extremely expensive and their usage is limited to BSL4 and other

103
00:10:58,670 --> 00:11:04,100
high containment facilities.

104
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:08,830
We now move on to the selection of the PPEs.

105
00:11:08,830 --> 00:11:14,990
The PPEs is based on the risk assessment.

106
00:11:14,990 --> 00:11:20,750
Now in the risk assessment we must consider the biological agent itself and the risk group

107
00:11:20,750 --> 00:11:23,670
to which it belongs.

108
00:11:23,670 --> 00:11:27,710
Now I have indicated another risk group here which is novel.

109
00:11:27,710 --> 00:11:34,790
So in case the risk group is unknown or novel, we must utilize the highest level of protection

110
00:11:34,790 --> 00:11:38,710
which involves the pressurized suit.

111
00:11:38,710 --> 00:11:47,350
The various risk groups and their PPEs associated with them are listed in this particular table.

112
00:11:47,350 --> 00:11:50,550
So we have respirators for risk group 1 and 2.

113
00:11:50,550 --> 00:11:56,510
However, in the case of the higher risk groups, it is recommended that a biosafety suit and

114
00:11:56,510 --> 00:12:05,150
a PAPR be utilized in conjunction with each other.

115
00:12:05,230 --> 00:12:09,630
We must also take into account the portal of entry when selecting the PPE.

116
00:12:09,630 --> 00:12:16,510
So in this case, we must select the PPE based on the route of entry or the portal of entry

117
00:12:16,510 --> 00:12:19,310
of the biological agent.

118
00:12:19,310 --> 00:12:26,390
I have given you this table and you can study it in order to determine the selection

119
00:12:26,390 --> 00:12:35,180
of the PPE based on the portal of entry.

120
00:12:35,180 --> 00:12:39,740
PPEs must also take into account laboratory procedures.

121
00:12:39,740 --> 00:12:45,260
For instance, a simple laboratory procedure such as receiving a sample may require donning

122
00:12:45,260 --> 00:12:50,540
or wearing of gloves, a respirator, and a biosafety suit.

123
00:12:50,540 --> 00:12:59,020
However, when you process the sample using PPEs which can generate an aerosol, we must

124
00:12:59,020 --> 00:13:05,320
use additional protection such as a PAPR.

125
00:13:05,320 --> 00:13:10,560
Certain procedures such as nucleic acid extraction or DNA and RNA extraction may

126
00:13:10,560 --> 00:13:17,040
also require usage of PAPR as the process of centrifugation and extraction can lead

127
00:13:17,040 --> 00:13:23,910
to the production of aerosols.

128
00:13:23,910 --> 00:13:28,470
Another challenge faced by laboratory workers is working with animals.

129
00:13:28,470 --> 00:13:38,470
Small animals tend to bite and scratch and this can lead to cutaneous wounds and biological

130
00:13:38,470 --> 00:13:43,110
agents can enter through these cuts and scratches in the skin.

131
00:13:43,110 --> 00:13:48,910
When we conduct a risk assessment for animals, we must look at these factors, the size of

132
00:13:48,910 --> 00:13:55,870
the animal, the behavior of the animal, the reservoir for potential human pathogens, ozonotic

133
00:13:55,870 --> 00:14:02,270
agents, the field work or laboratory work, and the portal of entry of the biological

134
00:14:02,270 --> 00:14:05,270
agent carried by the animals.

135
00:14:05,270 --> 00:14:11,430
Working with large animals may involve the selection and usage of specific PPEs.

136
00:14:15,670 --> 00:14:21,190
PPEs must be used in conjunction with administrative controls.

137
00:14:21,190 --> 00:14:25,590
The selection of PPEs must be done after thorough risk assessment.

138
00:14:25,590 --> 00:14:29,470
This in itself is an administrative control.

139
00:14:29,470 --> 00:14:34,550
The choice of PPEs depends on the risk group and the laboratory procedure which is another

140
00:14:34,550 --> 00:14:37,310
administrative control.

141
00:14:37,310 --> 00:14:43,150
Laboratory users must be trained to don and doff PPEs in compliance with SOPs.

142
00:14:43,150 --> 00:14:51,070
Doffing refers to wearing of a PPE and doffing refers to removal of a PPE.

143
00:14:51,070 --> 00:14:56,870
All incidents and accidents involving PPEs must be documented and reported during the audit.

144
00:14:56,870 --> 00:15:04,310
This includes tearing of the PPE during usage as well as the expiry date.

145
00:15:04,310 --> 00:15:13,390
Now, PPEs have an expiry date and a PPE should not be used after the expiry date.

146
00:15:13,390 --> 00:15:19,710
PPEs must be disposed in accordance with standard operating procedures for waste handling.

147
00:15:19,710 --> 00:15:29,000
This may involve the usage of autoclaves as well as incineration.

148
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:34,160
To summarize, the selection of PPEs is based on risk assessment.

149
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:37,680
We first look at the risk group.

150
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:42,560
The next aspect which we look at is the portal of entry.

151
00:15:42,560 --> 00:15:47,320
And finally, we look at the laboratory procedures.

152
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:57,280
Please note that all PPEs must be utilized in accordance with the standard operating procedures.

153
00:15:57,280 --> 00:16:00,560
That brings us to the end of this lecture on PPEs.

154
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:05,560
Thank you very much for watching and I wish you a pleasant learning experience.

155
00:16:05,560 --> 00:16:06,080
Thank you.
